+    <section class="journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+      <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <div class="journal-text" id="journal-text">
+        <p>Every bag we roast begins months before it reaches our shop, usually at a long wooden table in a cooperative
+          office a few hours from the nearest paved road. We buy directly from small farms and cooperatives in
+          Ethiopia, Colombia, Guatemala, and Sumatra, and we visit each of them at least once every two years. Walking
+          the rows, tasting cherries off the branch, and sitting down with the families who pick them tells us more
+          than any spec sheet ever could.</p>
+        <p>Back home, nothing gets bought on reputation alone. Producers send us small samples, and we roast each one
+          on a tiny sample roaster and taste it blind, a process called cupping. We score aroma, acidity, body,
+          sweetness, and finish, and only lots that score above 85 are even considered. Most weeks, that means we say
+          no far more often than yes.</p>
+        <p>Price matters just as much as flavor. We pay at least twice the commodity market rate, and we agree on the
+          price before the harvest rather than after, so farmers can plan for fertilizer, labor, and repairs without
+          gambling on the market. Whenever possible we sign multi-year agreements, because a relationship that lasts
+          lets a farm invest in better drying beds and processing equipment, and those investments show up in the cup.
+        </p>
+        <p>Once green coffee arrives at our port, it rests for a few weeks before we roast it in small batches of no
+          more than twenty kilograms. We log every roast curve and cup the result the next morning. If a batch is off,
+          we adjust or we don't sell it. Our goal is simple: to make sure that the person drinking your latte can taste
+          the care of every hand it passed through.</p>
+      </div>
+      <button type="button" class="btn summarize-btn" id="summarize-btn">Summarize</button>
+      <p class="summary-status" id="summary-status" role="status"></p>
+      <div class="summary" id="summary" hidden>
+        <ul id="summary-list"></ul>
+      </div>
+    </section>
